                  {
                    "id": "claude-event-2",
                    "phase": "item.completed",
                    "status": "completed",
                    "title": "item.completed",
                    "detail": "Workspace diff written. Result:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\n  \"summary\": \"Round 2, candidate-02: collapse the affine+relu chain from 4 GPU launches to 3 by folding the y-coefficient into torch.add's alpha and fusing the scale+shift into a single torch.lerp, then relu in place. Distinct direction from the rejected candidate-01, which only deleted the redundant .clone() and kept four elementwise kernels.\",\n  \"diagnosis\": {\n    \"summary\": \"At shape 32x256 (8192 FP32 elements) every kernel in this chain is far below launch cost, so p50 latency is a pure linear function of the number of aten kernels dispatched. The measured evidence fits an exact 2.56us-per-launch law: baseline 6 kernels -> 15.36us, candidate-01 4 kernels -> 10.24us. Neither memory bandwidth, occupancy, nor arithmetic is the limiter; the only lever is kernel count.\",\n    \"bottlenecks\": [\n      \"6 CUDA launches in naive_v0 (mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone) at ~2.56us each = 15.36us p50\",\n      \"candidate-01 removed only the clone: 4 launches = 10.24us, still ~10us above any achievable target\",\n      \"one aten dispatch + launch per elementwise op; 8192 elements of FP32 work is ~0 on this GPU\",\n      \"the task target (>=99.9999% relative improvement) implies a p50 of ~1.5e-5 us, which no real kernel launch can reach; no candidate can satisfy the Accept Gate metric\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"patch\": \"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -1,5 +1,45 @@\\n import torch\\n \\n+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n+# Optimized run(inputs) for  out = relu(x * 1.25 + y * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n+#\\n+#   out = relu(1.25 * x + 0.75 * y - 0.125)\\n+#       = relu(1.25 * (x + 0.6 * y) - 0.125)          since 0.75 / 1.25 == 0.6\\n+#\\n+#   1) acc = x + 0.6 * y            -> torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6)\\n+#   2) acc = 1.25 * acc - 0.125     -> torch.lerp(acc, 0.5, -0.25)\\n+#   3) out = relu(acc)              -> torch.relu_ (in place)\\n+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n+\\n+_LERP_END_CACHE = {}\\n+\\n+\\n+def _lerp_end(reference_tensor):\\n+    key = (reference_tensor.device, reference_tensor.dtype)\\n+    end = _LERP_END_CACHE.get(key)\\n+    if end is None:\\n+        end = torch.full((), 0.5, device=key[0], dtype=key[1])\\n+        _LERP_END_CACHE[key] = end\\n+    return end\\n+\\n+\\n def inputs_for(rows, cols):\\n@@ -22,6 +62,10 @@ def reference(inputs):\\n \\n def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+    x = inputs['x']\\n+    y = inputs['y']\\n+    # Kernel 1: x + 0.6 * y  (0.6 == 0.75 / 1.25).\\n+    acc = torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6)\\n+    # Kernel 2: 1.25 * acc - 0.125, fused into a single elementwise pass.\\n+    acc = torch.lerp(acc, _lerp_end(acc), -0.25)\\n+    # Kernel 3: relu, in place -> no extra allocation, no extra launch.\\n+    return torch.relu_(acc)\\n\",\n  \"candidates\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"candidate-02\",\n      \"title\": \"Three-launch affine+relu via alpha folding and lerp fusion\",\n      \"hypothesis\": \"Baseline and round-1 evidence both show a fixed ~2.56us cost per dispatched elementwise kernel (15.36us/6 launches, 10.24us/4 launches). Folding the y coefficient into torch.add's alpha argument and the 1.25*acc - 0.125 scale+shift into a single torch.lerp removes two launches and should land near 7.7us p50, about 50% below the naive_v0 baseline and 25% below candidate-01, while remaining bit-comparable within the 1e-5 tolerance.\",\n      \"change\": \"run(inputs) now executes exactly three CUDA kernels: (1) torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6) computes x + 0.6*y in one launch instead of two multiplies plus an add; (2) torch.lerp(acc, 0.5, -0.25) computes 1.25*acc - 0.125 in one launch, since lerp(a,b,w) = (1-w)*a + w*b and w=-0.25, b=0.5 gives exactly 1.25*a - 0.125; (3) torch.relu_ applies the nonlinearity in place. The 0-dim lerp endpoint is cached per (device, dtype) so no host-to-device copy occurs inside run(). get_inputs, get_test_cases, get_benchmark_inputs and reference are byte-identical to the baseline; no caller-owned tensor is mutated.\",\n      \"files\": [\"run.py\"],\n      \"sourceReferences\": [],\n      \"risks\": [\n        \"The float32 constant 0.6 is not exactly representable, so the y term carries a relative error near 4e-8; worst-case deviation at |y|MAX=5 is ~1.5e-7, far inside atol=rtol=1e-5, and round-1 already passed correctness with 9.5e-7 maxDiff from a different association order.\",\n        \"Reassociating to 1.25*(x + 0.6*y) changes rounding versus the reference's 1.25*x + 0.75*y; the tolerance margin is ~65x, so this is safe but is the main numerical assumption.\",\n        \"torch.lerp with a 0-dim endpoint relies on TensorIterator broadcasting; verified as supported, but it is the one op in the chain that is not a plain add/mul/relu.\",\n        \"Expected gain is ~7.7us p50. The stated 99.9999% target requires ~1.5e-5 us, which is below a single kernel launch; the Accept Gate performance rule is unreachable by any real CUDA implementation, so this candidate is expected to be recorded as a weak candidate again rather than adopted.\",\n        \"This candidate is a bounded three-kernel floor for eager PyTorch: the remaining affine cannot be fused further without torch.compile or CUDA graph capture, both of which add host-side replay overhead that would likely exceed the ~2.5us saved.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recommendedCandidate\": \"candidate-02\",\n  \"nextAction\": {\n    \"type\": \"candidate.plan\",\n    \"title\": \"If candidate-02 confirms the 2.56us/launch law, test whether launch overhead itself can be cut\",\n    \"reason\": \"candidate-02 tests the last legal reduction in kernel count available to eager PyTorch. If the measured p50 lands near 7.7us the launch-cost model is confirmed, and the only remaining lever is per-launch overhead rather than kernel count.\",\n    \"expectedOutput\": \"One bounded run.py diff that either keeps the three-kernel math but reduces dispatch cost, or a documented conclusion that the remaining gap to the 99.9999% target is physically unattainable on local-shared-gpu.\",\n    \"risk\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"risks\": [\n    \"Local correctness and benchmark execution is forbidden this turn; the 7.7us projection is derived from round-1 measured evidence (2.56us per launch), not from a run performed here.\",\n    \"The 99.9999% relative-improvement target is not reachable: it implies a p50 of roughly 1.5e-5 microseconds, below the cost of a single CUDA launch, so the Accept Gate performance rule cannot pass regardless of implementation quality.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
